  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Represent each condition as a set",
      "body": "<p>Translate “and” as intersection and “or” as union. Mark the allowed real inputs for each statement.</p>",
      "recap": "Inclusive OR allows either condition or both; AND requires both conditions together.",
      "pitfall": "Do not read “or” as exclusive.",
      "pause": "Draw the five sets on number lines.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l01",
          "reminder": "Inclusive OR allows either condition or both; AND requires both conditions together."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Count forced statements",
      "body": "<p>A condition is sufficient for another when every input allowed by the first is allowed by the second. Count those containments, excluding the statement itself.</p>",
      "recap": "Sufficiency means one condition guarantees the other in every case, not merely at one test value.",
      "pitfall": "Several statements true at one chosen input do not by themselves show an implication.",
      "pause": "For each candidate, count the other conditions it implies.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l04",
          "reminder": "Sufficiency means one condition guarantees the other in every case, not merely at one test value."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Treat each statement as its set of allowed real inputs: A is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; B is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; C is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; D is all real numbers; E is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. In D, the two ranges cover the whole real line.</p><p>A is sufficient for D only. B is sufficient for all four others. C is sufficient for A,D,E, but not B because 0 is allowed. D forces none of the other four. E is sufficient for A,D, but not B or C because it also contains interior values such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Exactly C guarantees three other statements. Answer C.</p>",
